<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data
</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_pubid"><span class="ltx_note_name">PubID: </span>pubid: 0000–0000/00$00.00 © 2021 IEEE</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qi Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiao Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mingyi Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuan Yuan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mengbai Xiao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Fuzhen Zhuang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">and Dongxiao Yu
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Qi Wang, Xiao Zhang, Mingyi Li, Mengbai Xiao, and Dongxiao Yu are with the School of Computer Science and Technology, Shandong University, Qingdao 266237, China (Email:
wsq5457@mail.sdu.edu.cn; xiaozhang@sdu.edu.cn; limee@mail.sdu.edu.cn; xiaomb@sdu.edu.cn; dxyu@sdu.edu.cn).</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Yuan Yuan is with the School of Software $&amp;$ Joint SDU-NTU Centre for Artificial Intelligence Research (C-FAIR), Shandong University, Jinan, 250000, China.˜Email: yyuan@sdu.edu.cn;</span></span></span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Fuzhen Zhuang is with the Institute of Artificial Intelligence, SKLSDE,
School of Computer Science, Beihang University, Beijing 100191, China (Email: zhuangfuzhen@buaa.edu.cn).</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">With the development of distributed systems, microservices and cloud native technologies have become central to modern enterprise software development. Despite bringing significant advantages, these technologies also increase system complexity and operational challenges. Traditional root cause analysis (RCA) struggles to achieve automated fault response, heavily relying on manual intervention. In recent years, large language models (LLMs) have made breakthroughs in contextual inference and domain knowledge integration, providing new solutions for Artificial Intelligence for Operations (AIOps).
However, Existing LLM-based approaches face three key challenges:
text input constraints, dynamic service dependency hallucinations, and context window limitations. To address these issues, we propose a tool-assisted LLM agent with multi-modality observation data, namely TAMO, for fine-grained RCA.
It unifies multi-modal observational data into time-aligned representations to extract consistent features and employs specialized root cause localization and fault classification tools for perceiving the contextual environment. This approach overcomes the limitations of LLM in handling real-time changing service dependencies and raw observational data and guides LLM to generate repair strategies aligned with system contexts by structuring key information into a prompt. Experimental results show that TAMO performs well in root cause analysis when dealing with public datasets characterized by heterogeneity and common fault types, demonstrating its effectiveness.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Root cause analysis, Tool-Assisted LLM agent, Cloud-native systems, Multimodal data, Diffusion.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years,
Microservice architecture and cloud-native technologies have become foundational elements in modern enterprise software development, driving the rapid development of distributed systems.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. Microservices decompose traditional monolithic applications into independent distributed services, while cloud native technologies leverage containerization and container orchestration<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> to enhance the capabilities for flexible resource allocation, dynamic scaling, and continuous delivery<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
In enterprise production environments, microservice systems are composed of numerous independent services and containerized entities<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, which significantly increased system complexity, These systems have intricate inter-service dependencies and communication patterns, where the failure of any component (e.g., network resource corruption) can trigger cascading effects that jeopardize overall system stability, potentially leading to significant economic losses<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. How to achieve accurate root cause identification and resolution in complex distributed systems has become a key factor in reducing production risks and minimizing losses for modern enterprises.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20462v1/intro1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/110;" width="229" height="110" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>A failure case in a cloud-native system demonstrates three major challenges when using LLM directly for root cause analysis.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Root cause analysis aims to quickly correlate related multiple entity features, identify the root cause and determine the corresponding fault type, and provide actionable solutions, thus realizing an automatic fault response mechanism and minimizing human intervention<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.
How to perform an accurate and efficient root cause analysis in the distributed environment has become an important research direction.
Traditional RCA methods typically rely on rule-based anomaly detection or statistical correlation analysis (e.g. causal graphs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>) to localize faults. These methods struggle to jointly analyze heterogeneous data modalities (e.g., unstructured logs and structured metrics), often leading to fragmented information and frequent error detection.
DL-based RCA methods attempt to incorporate multimodal feature fusion and dependency-sensitive Graph Neural Networks (GNNs)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> to achieve accurate fault localization. Although these methods demonstrate good performance in specific tasks such as fault localization in complex systems, they still face significant challenges in real-time analysis and fault interpretability, heavily relying on the expertise of engineers to complete the root cause analysis and remediation process.
Recently, large language models have demonstrated revolutionary capabilities in automated root cause analysis, opening new avenues for AIOps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. LLM can combine system faults with the current system context to generate actionable fault diagnosis reports, as evidenced by prototypes such as RCACopilot<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
Recent works such as RCAgent<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and mABC<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> developed LLM-driven automated RCA frameworks that enable accurate fault localization and provide reasonable repair suggestions, thus further advancing the field of automated operations and maintenance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, although existing LLM-based methods have significantly alleviated the reliance on expert knowledge in traditional DL-based methods, they still face the following three key challenges in real distributed system environments:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Context Limitations</span>: Limited by fixed-length context windows, the LLM is unable to effectively process massive amounts of contextual monitoring data in real-time. This constraint often leads to data truncation or compression during analysis, making it difficult to utilize historical information to detect anomalous trends in system components, thereby affecting the rationale and accuracy of root cause analysis. <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">So how to extract useful information from the complex system context and input it into the context-limited LLM is a key challenge.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text Input Constraints</span>: Existing large language models such as ChatGPT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, DeepSeek<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, Qwen<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, etc., either only support text data as input or enable modality input by mapping different modal features to a unified textual semantic space. However, due to the lack of obvious correlation between time series and text, this transformation process often results in a significant loss of key feature information, severely hindering the analytical and reasoning capabilities of LLM. <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">So how to efficiently unify multiple modalities of observation data to assist the LLM in providing accurate root cause analysis results is a key challenge.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Dependence Hallucination</span>: The LLM is based on static, pre-trained knowledge, which makes it challenging to model service dependencies in systems. It can only simulate simple graph structures using text prompts. As noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, LLMs struggle with reasoning over complex graphs, which limits their ability to effectively capture service dependencies. Additionally, the dynamic nature of service call chains in the system means that evolving dependencies can cause the LLM to generate hallucinations that contradict actual dependencies, leading to inaccurate root cause analysis. <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">So how to assist the LLM in modeling fault propagation paths using call chain relationships to achieve fine-grained root cause localization is a key challenge.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose a tool-assisted LLM agent framework based on domain-specific perceptive models.
This framework decouples the LLM from the raw observational data by utilizing specialized observational data analysis tools(<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>), addressing the constraints of text input and the limitations of context in the LLM.
These specialized model tools are used to perceive the contextual environment, and their results form structured text inputs that are fed back to the RCA expert agent for further analysis, generating accurate root cause analysis results and reasonable maintenance recommendations.
Specifically, to address the problem of multimodal data alignment, we propose a dual-branch diffusion model as the data feature observation tool (<math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), which unifies multimodal observability data (logs, traces, metrics) into temporally consistent representations. To address the issue of dynamic dependencies, the root cause localization tool (<math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) performs a joint analysis of entity features and call topologies, modeling the fault propagation chain, and identifying the root cause through frequency-domain attention-driven temporal causal analysis. Subsequently, the fault classification tool (<math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>) further analyzes the fault and maps the symptoms to predefined fault types.
The main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a tool-assisted LLM agent framework, which integrates domain-specific model tools with LLMs for root cause analysis of microservices. This framework enables the LLM to analyze the system state in real time by calling perception tools, decoupling the agent from the raw observational data, allowing it to generate accurate root cause analysis results and reasonable maintenance recommendations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a dual-branch collaborative diffusion tool that captures potentially consistent patterns across modalities and provides a unified time series representation for downstream analysis tasks through condition-oriented collaborative reconstruction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a root cause localization tool based on frequency-domain attention mechanisms, which identifies causal dependency patterns in frequency-domain features while filtering out low-frequency noise, achieving precise root cause localization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments on two benchmark datasets demonstrate that TAMO outperforms all compared methods including state-of-the-art (SOTA) approaches and derived baselines. Ablation and case studies further validate the effectiveness of the framework.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In modern cloud native systems, with the widespread adoption of microservice architectures and dynamic infrastructures, it has become particularly important to ensure high system availability and rapid fault recovery, making root cause analysis (RCA) a critical part of operations and maintenance. As a result, various RCA methods have been developed to guarantee system reliability, including traditional RCA methods, multi-modal RCA methods, and LLM-based RCA methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Traditional RCA methods</span>: Traditional RCA methods are mainly based on statistical correlation analysis techniques, such as anomaly detection based on predefined rules<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> and root cause localization strategies based on causal discovery<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. While these approaches perform well in static environments, they have difficulties in adapting to the dynamic service dependencies that characterize cloud native environments.
With the rapid advancement of deep learning, neural network-based methods for real-time system data analysis continue to emerge. DeepLog<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> models log features via long short-term memory networks; GDN<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and GTA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> construct graph neural networks using metrics data to capture fault propagation paths; Sage<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> leverages causal Bayesian networks with trace data for root cause localization. However, these approaches predominantly focus on unimodal data, failing to exploit complementary inter-modal relationships.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-modal RCA methods</span>: The multi-modal-based approach significantly reduces the missed detection rate by comprehensively considering system observation data from different modalities. Due to the differences between modalities, it is crucial to extract the correlated features across different data. PDiagnose<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> analyzes different modality data as independent entities and uses a voting mechanism to identify the root cause entity. Although this method successfully utilizes data from multiple modalities, it overlooks the consistency relationships between features from different modalities. Eadro<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, performs gated fusion of features extracted from different modalities and uses a graph neural network to fuse embedded features at the service level for root cause localization. HolisticRCA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, compared to Eadro, fully considers the heterogeneous nature of microservice systems and standardizes the embedding of different modality data using an assembling building blocks strategy, combining masked embeddings for overall root cause analysis. In contrast to directly using raw data for root cause localization, Nezha<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and DiagFusion<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> convert heterogeneous multimodal data into homogeneous event representations, performing joint analysis through event graphs to achieve root cause localization. CoE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> further supports the operational experience of site reliability engineers as input on the event graph, enhancing the understanding and interpretability of the event. While these methods significantly improve the accuracy of root cause localization through multimodal fusion and comprehensive system analysis, their root cause analysis process still relies on human intervention by engineers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-based RCA methods</span>: Due to the advanced reasoning and contextual understanding capabilities of large language models, LLM-based RCA methods have become significant advancements in automated root cause analysis. Currently, LLM-based RCA methods are still in the early stages of development. RCAgent<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> handles anomalies through a tool-augmented agent framework, while mABC<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> employs a blockchain-based multi-agent system for root cause voting and solution formulation. However, directly applying LLMs to RCA presents several critical challenges: the limited context window of LLMs restricts the processing of large-scale observation data, and due to text input constraints, they are unable to effectively handle time-series data (e.g., metrics), resulting in the loss of important feature information. Additionally, another key issue is how to use large models to capture and model dynamic dependencies in cloud native systems in real-time, without introducing misleading or false conclusions. These challenges highlight the need for further innovations in integrating LLM with multimodal and heterogeneous data sources for more accurate root cause analysis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20462v1/framework.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:419/258;" width="419" height="258" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>The proposed TAMO framework consists of a Data Feature Observation Tool (<math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), a Root Cause Localization Tool (<math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), a Fault Classification Tool (<math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>), and an RCA Expert Agent (<math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math>). In the framework, the agent <math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math> calls tools <math id="S2.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> as perception tools to analyze the system contextual observational data in real time. The results of these perceptions are structured into text inputs that are fed back into the agent. By analyzing the perception results, the expert agent will provide the corresponding root cause analysis and repair suggestions.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">METHODOLOGY</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To fully exploit the multimodal observability data of cloud native systems while integrating prior domain knowledge, we propose <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TAMO</span>, <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>ool-Assisted LLM <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>gents that Integrates <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ulti-Modality
<span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>bservation Data to address root cause entity localization and fault classification in large-scale system operations and maintenance. This workflow utilizes observation tools composed of a dual-branch collaborative diffusion model to effectively extract and integrate multimodal data from different entities. Additionally, task-specialized tools explicitly model the complex dependencies among entities using a causal graph, with feature propagation through the causal graph enabling accurate root cause analysis. Furthermore, to fully incorporate domain knowledge, we employ a Large Language Model (LLM) with operational expertise as an expert agent.
The expert agent <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math> integrates the outputs from the preceding tools <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with context knowledge of the system to offer human engineers a comprehensive fault analysis and recommended solutions.
As shown in Fig.<a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II Related Work ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the proposed workflow consists of three tools and one agent, <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, each responsible for a specific task in the root cause analysis process:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data Feature Observation Tool (<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>): This tool employs a dual-branch collaborative diffusion model to integrate multimodal features. It reconstructs and fuses textual data features, such as logs, into time-series data based on conditional guidance;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Root Cause Localization Tool (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>): Utilizing the multimodal-enhanced time-series data as input, this tool models causal relationships between entities using a frequency-domain self-attention module, enabling accurate fault root cause localization;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fault Classification Tool (<math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>): Based on the causal relationships between entities and the invocation chain structure, this tool classifies the types of faults;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RCA Expert Agent (<math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math>): Leveraging the expertise of a Large Language Model (LLM), this agent synthesizes the outputs from previous tools along with system environment context to provide site reliability engineers (SREs) with a comprehensive fault analysis and recommended solutions.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Data Feature Observation Tool (<math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models are a class of generative models based on Markov processes that have demonstrated exceptional performance in the generation of data in various domains, including images<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, voice<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and time series<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. The core idea is to gradually add noise to the data through a forward denoising (forward diffusion) process, which converts the original data distribution to an approximate Gaussian distribution. In the reverse denoising process, the model learns how to gradually remove the noise to generate the desired data. In this tool, we use log patterns and temporal features as control conditions for two diffusion branches. During the denoising process, the two branches collaborate to guide the generation of time-series data with multimodal features from the noise-perturbed original data.
Specifically, during the forward diffusion process, the original data <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim q(x_{0})\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times m\times l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim q(x_{0})\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times m\times l}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> entities, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> channels, and a time series of length <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. This process can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\left(x_{1},\cdots,x_{T}\mid x_{0}\right):=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q\left(x_{t}\mid x_{t-1}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(x_{1},\cdots,x_{T}\mid x_{0}\right):=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q\left(x_{t}\mid x_{t-1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a predefined noise scaling factors that determines how much information from the previous step is retained, then each step of the Eq.<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III-A Data Feature Observation Tool (𝒯_1) ‣ III METHODOLOGY ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\left(1-\alpha_{t}\right)\mathbf{I}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t};\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\left(1-\alpha_{t}\right)\mathbf{I}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">After <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps of noise addition, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to random noise <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, which approximates an Gaussian distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we perform stepwise denoising on the noisy data using a dual-branch conditional mechanism. The parameterized conditional transition distribution can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathcal{C}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathcal{C}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t-1};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right),\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right)\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t-1};\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right),\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{0:T},\mathcal{C}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{0:T},\mathcal{C}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p\left(\mathbf{x}_{T}\right)\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathcal{C}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p\left(\mathbf{x}_{T}\right)\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t-1}\mid\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathcal{C}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{c_{log},c_{time}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{c_{log},c_{time}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of control conditions, with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{log}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{time}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{time}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the log branch and the time-series branch, respectively. These conditions guide the denoising process, ensuring that the generated data have multi-modal features by utilizing both log-based patterns and temporal features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the generation of the log-conditioned variable <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{log}</annotation></semantics></math>, since unstructured log representations are difficult to extract high-quality features, inspired by previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, we extract event patterns <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and keywords <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> from the raw logs using Drain<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and by calculating the TF-IDF scores, we select the highest scoring pattern-keyword pairs as the conditional variable <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{log}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{log}=\left\{\arg\max_{e\in\mathcal{E}}\operatorname{TF-IDF}(e)\right\}\cup\left\{\arg\max_{w\in\mathcal{W}}\operatorname{TF-IDF}(w)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>TF</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>IDF</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>TF</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>IDF</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{log}=\left\{\arg\max_{e\in\mathcal{E}}\operatorname{TF-IDF}(e)\right\}\cup\left\{\arg\max_{w\in\mathcal{W}}\operatorname{TF-IDF}(w)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the generation of the time-conditioned variable <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{time}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{time}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract key performance indicators (KPIs) such as latency and duration from trace files. These KPIs <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{kpi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{kpi}</annotation></semantics></math> are then embedded into a latent space using a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) network, enabling the model to effectively incorporate temporal characteristics into the denoising process.</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{time}=\operatorname{Embedding}\left(x_{kpi}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{h_{{time}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Embedding</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{time}=\operatorname{Embedding}\left(x_{kpi}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{h_{{time}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">During training, we use a dual-branch network to learn the reverse diffusion process, gradually denoising the noisy data. In this process, the noise term estimated at each step is collaboratively generated by the two branches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{X}_{t-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\mathbf{X}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{X}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right)\right)+\sigma_{t}\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{X}_{t-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\mathbf{X}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{X}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right)\right)+\sigma_{t}\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">in which, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{t}=\prod_{s=1}^{t}\alpha_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{t}=\prod_{s=1}^{t}\alpha_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cumulative product of the noise scaling factors <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the noise term predicted by the model, which is jointly generated based on the computations from the log branch <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{log}</annotation></semantics></math> and time-series branch <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{time}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{time}</annotation></semantics></math>. A hyperparameter <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is used as a mixing coefficient to balance the contributions of the two branches.</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right)=\mu\epsilon_{log}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathbf{c}_{log}\right)+\left(1-\mu\right)\epsilon_{time}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathbf{c}_{time}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathcal{C}\right)=\mu\epsilon_{log}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathbf{c}_{log}\right)+\left(1-\mu\right)\epsilon_{time}\left(\mathbf{x}_{t},t,\mathbf{c}_{time}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The original denoising diffusion model employs U-Net as the denoising backbone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. However, considering the characteristics of time-series data, we adopt a patch-based Transformer network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> as the backbone for each branch to better capture the representations of time-series subsequences. The training objective of this model is to minimize the KL-divergence between the distributions <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the objective derivation is formulated as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{diff}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{0},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}\left[\left\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t},t,\mathcal{C})\right\|_{2}^{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{diff}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{0},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}\left[\left\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t},t,\mathcal{C})\right\|_{2}^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Root Cause Localization Tool (<math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through the dual-branch diffusion model of Tool <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain time-series data that integrates multimodal features. However, since this process operates at the level of system resource entities, the generated time-series <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{ent}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_{ent}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{ent}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_{ent}}</annotation></semantics></math> maintains the same dimensionality as the original resource entities, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ent" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ent</annotation></semantics></math> represents the corresponding resource entity, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the time window length, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ent}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ent}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the feature dimension associated with each entity. Due to the heterogeneity of observability data across different resource entities in cloud-native systems, inconsistencies exist among them, which hinders unified learning in the model. To address this issue, we first perform data embedding on the features of different entities, mapping them into a unified dimensional space for consistency:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{ent}=\operatorname{DataEmbedding}\left(x^{\prime}_{ent}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_{model}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>DataEmbedding</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{ent}=\operatorname{DataEmbedding}\left(x^{\prime}_{ent}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_{model}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{model}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{model}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the unified embedding dimension. After mapping all entity features into the latent space, we concatenate the output results to obtain the embedded vector <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{emb}\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times L\times d_{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{emb}\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times L\times d_{model}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the unified embedding vector <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{emb}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider that root cause localization tasks typically require the detection and identification of short-term anomalies or disturbance signals within the system entity. These transient features may be obscured by smoothed trends in the time domain. To address this issue, we apply the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) to convert the data from the time domain to the frequency domain. Formally, the transformation can be expressed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}\left(H^{emb}\right)=\operatorname{FFT}\left(H^{emb}\right)=\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}H^{emb}_{i}e^{-j2\pi ki/K},\quad" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>FFT</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\left(H^{emb}\right)=\operatorname{FFT}\left(H^{emb}\right)=\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}H^{emb}_{i}e^{-j2\pi ki/K},\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e^{-j2\pi ki/N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-j2\pi ki/N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complex exponential Fourier basis function, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{emb}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{emb}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the time-series value corresponding to the time step <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,\ldots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,\ldots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Considering the real-time demands of root cause localization, the model needs to focus on short-term fluctuations in the observed data. To achieve this, we apply a high-pass filter to the frequency-domain data, retaining the first <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> high-frequency components which helps to amplify the anomalous signals within the data. Formally, this can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Indices}=\operatorname{argtopk}\left(\left|\mathcal{F}\left(H^{emb}\right)\right|,k=freq\_topk\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Indices</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>argtopk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Indices}=\operatorname{argtopk}\left(\left|\mathcal{F}\left(H^{emb}\right)\right|,k=freq\_topk\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Mask=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{ if }k\in\text{ Indices }\\
0,&amp;\text{ otherwise }\end{cases},k=0,1,\ldots,K-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mtext> Indices </mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise </mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Mask=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{ if }k\in\text{ Indices }\\
0,&amp;\text{ otherwise }\end{cases},k=0,1,\ldots,K-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}=\mathcal{F}\left(H^{emb}\right)\odot Mask\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times K\times d_{model}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>filtered </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}=\mathcal{F}\left(H^{emb}\right)\odot Mask\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times K\times d_{model}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since the interactions between microservices in the cloud native system can be described by a dependency graph, in order to fully consider the relationships between entities in the system and capture potential fault propagation pathways, we use the self-attention mechanism to construct a causal dependency graph in the frequency domain. The process can be defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=Softmax\left(Attention(\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}W^{Q},\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}W^{K})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>filtered </mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>filtered </mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=Softmax\left(Attention(\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}W^{Q},\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}W^{K})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> are the query and key matrices respectively. Subsequently, to reduce the influence of irrelevant entities and remove redundant relationships, we introduce topological constraints into the graph generation process. These constraints can be defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\text{maske}}=\mathbf{A}\odot\left(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{I}_{E}\right)\odot\operatorname{TopkMask}(\cdot,k=topk)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>maske</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>TopkMask</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\text{maske}}=\mathbf{A}\odot\left(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{I}_{E}\right)\odot\operatorname{TopkMask}(\cdot,k=topk)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">in which <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entity diagonal matrix for avoiding self-loops and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TopkMask}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>TopkMask</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TopkMask}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the top <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most significant dependencies for each entity. Then, we apply graph neural network to perform root cause inference and localization. Specifically, we select the Graph Attention Network (GAT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> as the backbone for aggregating neighborhood feature information among system entities, in order to simulate the fault propagation process as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{s+1}_{i}=Relu\left(\alpha_{ii}W\mathcal{H}^{s}_{j}+\sum_{j\in N(i)}\alpha_{ij}W\mathcal{H}^{s}_{j}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{s+1}_{i}=Relu\left(\alpha_{ii}W\mathcal{H}^{s}_{j}+\sum_{j\in N(i)}\alpha_{ij}W\mathcal{H}^{s}_{j}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(i)=\{j|A_{mask}[i,j]=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(i)=\{j|A_{mask}[i,j]=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{s}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{s}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the representation of entity <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}_{i}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>filtered </mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}_{i}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{filtered }}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the attention coefficient between system entity <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and its related entity <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be calculated by the following formula:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi(i,j)=LeakyRelu\left(a^{T}\left(W^{s}\cdot h^{s}_{i}\oplus W^{s}\cdot h^{s}_{j}\right)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi(i,j)=LeakyRelu\left(a^{T}\left(W^{s}\cdot h^{s}_{i}\oplus W^{s}\cdot h^{s}_{j}\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ij}=\frac{\operatorname{exp}(\xi(i,j))}{\sum_{p\in N_{i}\cup\{i\}}\operatorname{exp}(\xi(i,p))}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ij}=\frac{\operatorname{exp}(\xi(i,j))}{\sum_{p\in N_{i}\cup\{i\}}\operatorname{exp}(\xi(i,p))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the concatenation operation, <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the vector of attention parameters and <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight matrix for the <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer. Finally, we use a max-pooling layer to aggregate the importance of instances, thereby obtaining the root cause probability for the system entities:</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\text{ MaxPool}\left(\mathbf{H}^{S}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times 1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext> MaxPool</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\text{ MaxPool}\left(\mathbf{H}^{S}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times 1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Considering that multiple types of entities may experience faults simultaneously within the same time period, meaning that the root cause entity may not be unique, we use binary cross-entropy (BCE) as the loss function to optimize the model. Specifically, for each label <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the model predicted value <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{0,1,...,E-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{0,1,...,E-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> represent the total number of system entities) , the loss function is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{BCE}=-\frac{1}{E}\sum_{i=1}^{E}\left[y_{i}\log(\hat{y_{i}})+(1-y_{i})\log(1-\hat{y_{i}})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>E</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{BCE}=-\frac{1}{E}\sum_{i=1}^{E}\left[y_{i}\log(\hat{y_{i}})+(1-y_{i})\log(1-\hat{y_{i}})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fault Classification Tool (<math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the root cause entity localization is performed by Tool <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the fault diagnosis process typically requires further identification of the specific fault type of the root cause entity (e.g., hardware overload, resource leakage, or configuration errors) to guide precise remediation strategies. Although root cause localization narrows down the scope of fault analysis (i.e., determining “where” the anomaly occurs), in practical operational scenarios, different fault types often correspond to differentiated handling strategies (e.g., memory leaks require service restarts, while CPU contention necessitates resource scaling). Therefore, this tool uses the time series data <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{ent}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{ent}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by tool <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (for simplicity, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>) to train a fault classification model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To model the latent temporal dependencies in the input time-series data <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, we use a Transformer model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> as the encoder network. The output of its single-head self-attention mechanism is given by:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Attention}=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{zW^{Q}\cdot z{W^{K}}^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)zW^{V}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Attention</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>W</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Attention}=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{zW^{Q}\cdot z{W^{K}}^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)zW^{V},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are linear projection matrices. Subsequently, we concatenate the results of the multi-head attention to obtain the latent vector representation:</p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\text{MultiHead}\left(zW^{Q},zW^{K},zW^{V}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MultiHead</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\text{MultiHead}\left(zW^{Q},zW^{K},zW^{V}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">After capturing the internal temporal dependencies of the time-series data, we employ the GAT model to capture the spatial dependencies between the time steps. According to formulas Eq.<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III-B Root Cause Localization Tool (𝒯_2) ‣ III METHODOLOGY ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>-Eq.<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III-B Root Cause Localization Tool (𝒯_2) ‣ III METHODOLOGY ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>, we perform feature propagation on the latent vector representation. The representation of each entity is obtained by aggregating the weighted features of its neighboring entities:</p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}=\text{ELU}\left(\text{GATConv}(X,A)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ELU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mtext>GATConv</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}=\text{ELU}\left(\text{GATConv}(X,A)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where ELU is the activation function and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{E\times E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dependency between entities. Subsequently, we use an MLP network to output the fault type for the corresponding entity. Considering the issues of multi-class overlap and class imbalance in the fault classification task, we adopt a weighted binary cross-entropy (wBCE) loss function with the introduction of class weight coefficients <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as the model’s training objective:</p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{wBCE}=-\frac{1}{E}\sum_{i=1}^{E}\left[\lambda_{w}\cdot y_{i}\log(\hat{y_{i}})+(1-y_{i})\log(1-\hat{y_{i}})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>E</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{wBCE}=-\frac{1}{E}\sum_{i=1}^{E}\left[\lambda_{w}\cdot y_{i}\log(\hat{y_{i}})+(1-y_{i})\log(1-\hat{y_{i}})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">in which <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the ratio of negative to positive samples within each fault type class, which is used to enhance the loss contribution of the samples. Additionally, to prevent overfitting, we include an L2 weight decay term in the loss function. The final loss function can thus be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{wBCE}+\beta\left||\mathbf{W}\right||^{2}_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{wBCE}+\beta\left||\mathbf{W}\right||^{2}_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a predefined hyperparameter and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is the model weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RCA Expert Agent (<math id="S3.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the three tools <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> have completed tasks such as data feature alignment, root cause localization, and fault classification, the workflow has achieved a relatively deep understanding of the fault. However, relying solely on these outputs makes it difficult to provide comprehensive and accurate failure analysis and remediation plans. Therefore, to further improve fault response and system recovery efficiency, we introduce the RCA expert agent <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using large language models (LLM), this agent synthesizes the predictions from the previous tools and incorporates the current context of cloud-native microservice architecture to offer a more comprehensive and accurate fault diagnosis and resolution strategy for site reliability engineers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we use the GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> as the expert model, which gathers the outputs from the previous tools as background knowledge, including:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Root Cause Localization Results</span> (from <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>): Identifying the root cause location of the fault and the associated system components.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fault Classification Results</span> (from <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>): Providing the specific type of fault, such as network failure, hardware failure, configuration errors, etc.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System Context Information</span>: Including the system microservice architecture, dependencies between services, call chains, resource usage, and other related details, which provide essential context for fault diagnosis.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="801.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 801.67" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,801.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#595959;" fill="#595959" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 795.77 C 0 799.03 2.64 801.67 5.91 801.67 L 471.47 801.67 C 474.73 801.67 477.38 799.03 477.38 795.77 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 777.71 L 475.41 777.71 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 786.31)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">RCA Expert Prompt</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 85.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:49.16em;--ltx-fo-depth:5.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="752.13" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 680.26)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are an RCA expert responsible for analyzing and diagnosing failures of complex systems based on cloud-native, microservices architectures. You have access to detailed failure reports, system performance data, and historical failure patterns. You provide detailed analysis based on the following structured inputs, which include root cause information, failure classification, system architecture context, historical failure data, and other system state details. You are expected to apply advanced reasoning methods, drawing on knowledge of system architecture, historical failure modes, and industry best practices, to provide comprehensive, practical solutions to improve system reliability and minimize downtime.</span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[Failure root cause localization]</span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Root Entities: <span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;">&lt;localization results from <math id="S3.F3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;" class="ltx_font_mathscript" mathcolor="#8C0000">𝒯</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;" mathcolor="#8C0000">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>&gt;</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Related Entities: <span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;">&lt;the related calling entities&gt;</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[Fault Classification]</span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Fault Type: <span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;">&lt;classification results from <math id="S3.F3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;" class="ltx_font_mathscript" mathcolor="#8C0000">𝒯</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;" mathcolor="#8C0000">3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>&gt;</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Symptoms: <span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;">&lt;detailed Symptom Description&gt;</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[System architecture background]</span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Architecture: <span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;">&lt;microservice functionality&gt;</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">- Context: <span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C0000;">&lt;related logs and trace information&gt;</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[Task Objective]</span></span>
<span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F3.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Based on the information above, please analyze the root cause, impact scope, and proposed solutions for the fault, and provide the following:</span></span>
<span id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<span id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<span id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Analysis of the root cause of the fault.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<span id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Analysis of the type and level of failure.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<span id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Repair solutions and preventive measures.</span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Prompt templates for this RCA expert agent.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we prepare the prompt in the format shown in Fig.<a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-D RCA Expert Agent (𝒜) ‣ III METHODOLOGY ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> as input to the model, and the expected output is a detailed fault analysis report along with corresponding solution recommendations. Such outputs assist the SRE team in quickly understanding the root cause, impact scope, and taking targeted remediation actions, thus enhancing system reliability and response efficiency.</p>
</div>
