<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>3 </span>Evaluation metrics</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TAMO is dedicated to locating root cause instances and identifying fault types. Based on previous research works<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, we selected different evaluation metrics to evaluate the performance of model for these two tasks. Specifically, for the root cause localization task, we use top-k accuracy (AC@K) as the evaluation metric, which represents the probability that the true root cause entity appears within the top-k results produced by the method. Here, we set <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\{1,3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\{1,3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as the set of faults, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RC</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground truth root cause, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rc[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rc[k]</annotation></semantics></math> are the top-k root causes generated by the model. Therefore, AC@K can be defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AC@k=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{i\in S}^{S}\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }RC_{i}\in rc_{i}[k]\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AC@k=\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{i\in S}^{S}\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }RC_{i}\in rc_{i}[k]\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For the fault classification task, we use <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Precision(Pre)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Precision(Pre)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Recall(Rec)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Recall(Rec)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1-score(F1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1-score(F1)</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate performance. Given true positives (TP), false positives (FP), and false negatives (FN), the formulas for these metrics are as follows: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pre=\frac{TP}{TP+FP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pre=\frac{TP}{TP+FP}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rec=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rec=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1=\frac{2\cdot Pre\cdot Rec}{Pre+Rec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1=\frac{2\cdot Pre\cdot Rec}{Pre+Rec}</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering that this task is a multi-label classification problem, we adopt both micro and macro metrics for a comprehensive evaluation. Micro metrics aggregate the performance across all labels, treating all instances equally, which is useful when the class distribution is imbalanced. In contrast, macro metrics compute the metric for each label independently and then average the results, giving equal weight to each label regardless of its frequency. Thus, the final performance is measured using micro-precision (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MiPr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MiPr</annotation></semantics></math>), macro-precision (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MaPr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MaPr</annotation></semantics></math>), micro-recall (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MiRe" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MiRe</annotation></semantics></math>), macro-recall (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MaRe" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MaRe</annotation></semantics></math>), micro-F1 score (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MiF1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MiF1</annotation></semantics></math>), and macro-F1 score (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MaF1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MaF1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>4 </span>Implementation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implemented TAMO using Python torch 2.4.0 and CUDA 12.1. The experiments were conducted on a Linux server equipped with an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU, using Python 3.8. The model was trained with the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, and all experiments used a fixed random seed. For hyperparameters, we set the batch size to 32, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.5, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.001, and the initial learning rate to 0.001. Additionally, due to the heterogeneity of system entities in Dataset <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, which includes three levels: Pod, Service, and Node, the collected data features vary across these levels, making it challenging to train certain baselines uniformly. For such baselines, we partition Dataset <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> into subsets composed of different entity types (e.g., <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) and perform separate training and testing for each subset to evaluate their performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span><span id="S4.SS3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Effectiveness in Root Cause Localization</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ III-D RCA Expert Agent (𝒜) ‣ III METHODOLOGY ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> presents the results for root cause localization. The experimental data shows that our proposed TAMO model achieves the best or second-best performance across all datasets. Specifically, on the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> dataset, the score of TAMO exceeds the second-best score by at least 6%, and even in the second-best performance case, the gap with the best result is kept under 3%, clearly outperforming other competitors. Furthermore, we observe that the Eadro and Transformer models, which perform well on the B dataset, experience a significant drop in performance on heterogeneous datasets such as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because these two methods can only handle homogeneous entity features (e.g., in the dataset B, there is only one type of service entity). When confronted with heterogeneous entity features that need to be processed separately, their performance significantly deteriorates due to the lack of inter-entity correlation information. In contrast, both HolisticRCA and our approach model heterogeneous entities in a unified manner, maintaining strong performance even on the dataset A. Compared to HolisticRCA, we map multimodal information into time-series features and further optimize it through spatiotemporal modeling, achieving better performance in the root cause localization task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span><span id="S4.SS4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Effectiveness in Fault Classification</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the fault classification task, as shown in Table 2, TAMO demonstrates significant advantages in two key metrics: Micro Precision (MiPr) and Macro F1 (MaF1). Particularly on the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> dataset, TAMO achieves MaPr of 0.8869 and MaF1 of 0.8706, marking improvements of 24.32% and 19.85% respectively over the second-best model(HolisticRCA). TAMO stands out across all metrics such as Precision, Recall, and F1. Furthermore, it exhibits remarkable performance in the classification of complex fault types at the node level. In addition, TAMO excels in the precision metric, with MiPr reaching 0.7164 and 0.7182 on <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, surpassing the best baseline model by 8. 09% and 12. 69%, significantly reducing the risk of misclassification. Although MiPr on the smaller dataset <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is lower than the baseline model LightGBM, TAMO still outperforms LightGBM in terms of MaPr and F1 metrics. This suggests that TAMO performs more evenly across different classes, achieving better classification results for minority classes with fewer samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20462v1/case.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/84;" width="229" height="84" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>In case study experiments, two types of faults were induced in microservices, with the root cause entities identified as the Currency Service and CheckoutPod0.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span><span id="S4.SS5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Ablation Study</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To explore the effectiveness of each component in our TAMO framework, we designed three variant models and conducted ablation experiments. The variant models are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;\mathscr{T}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>TAMO</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;\mathscr{T}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>: To investigate the effectiveness of the diffusion method used by tool <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in fusing multimodal feature, in this variant we removed the tool <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and directly used the raw time series data as input to evaluate the variant performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;branch}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>TAMO</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;branch}</annotation></semantics></math>: To investigate the effectiveness of the dual-branch diffusion model in tool <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we removed the branch <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{time}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{time}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is responsible for maintaining temporal features, in this variant. Only the log branch <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{log}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{log}</annotation></semantics></math> was used in the diffusion reconstruction process. The reconstructed data were then used for fault classification to evaluate variant performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;FFT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>TAMO</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;FFT}</annotation></semantics></math>: To investigate the effectiveness of the frequency domain-based root cause localization method proposed for tool <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we removed the Fourier process in this variant. Instead, the original time-domain data were directly used for subsequent causal graph generation and graph convolution processes. The root cause localization results were then output to evaluate variant performance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20462v1/LLM.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/244;" width="467" height="244"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Comparison of LLM results between RCA analysis of Agent <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}</annotation></semantics></math> enhanced with TAMO tools and root cause analysis performed directly on raw data</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-B2 Baselines ‣ IV-B Experiment Setup ‣ IV Evaluation ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> presents the results of the ablation experiments, from which it is evident that the removal of any component in TAMO leads to performance degradation. This demonstrates that each of the component within TAMO has an important effect. In particular, the variant of TAMO without tool <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> shows a significant decrease in performance. This is because the fact that the variant <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;\mathscr{T}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>TAMO</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TAMO}_{w/o\;\mathscr{T}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> lacks the ability of the diffusion model to fuse multimodal information, thereby being reduced to learning with only a single modal feature. This absence of key feature information makes accurate root cause analysis challenging. Similarly, removing the time branch <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{time}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{time}</annotation></semantics></math> led to noticeable decreases in performance for both tasks, indicating that time series features are crucial for accurate fault localization and classification. Eliminating the FFT process resulted in a substantial degradation in RE localization metrics, demonstrating that frequency domain analysis is essential for precise root cause localization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">IV-F</span> </span><span id="S4.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4:</span><span id="S4.SS6.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Case Study</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the root cause analysis capabilities of TAMO in cloud-native ecosystems, this study conducts a case investigation using real-world failure data from HipsterShop. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-D RQ2: Effectiveness in Fault Classification ‣ IV Evaluation ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the microservices system is deployed using a Kubernetes (K8s) architecture, with service components developed in multiple programming languages. The system entry is managed through the Frontend service, and each service node is configured with three Pod replicas. Monitoring data collection points are set at both the service and Pod levels. Experimental data shows that TAMO demonstrates precise root cause identification for the current failure scenario: the root cause localization confidence for the Currency Service reaches 97.96% (service level), while the confidence for CheckoutPod0 is 82.39% (Pod level). Notably, while the overall Checkout service is functioning correctly, only Pod0 experiences issues. In contrast, the Currency service suffers from a global failure at the service level, causing anomalies in Pods 0-2. This comparative case indicates that TAMO effectively integrates multi-entity features and topological causal relationships to achieve precise root cause diagnosis across multiple layers. In the fault classification task, we perform a visual analysis of observation data for the anomalous services/Pods: CheckoutPod0 is accurately classified as a CPU overload fault due to a significant spike in core metrics such as ”CPU Usage Second.” The Currency service exhibits a correlation between increased service-level response time and decreased request success rate, while its Pod-level Receive Packet count continues to decline. TAMO successfully identifies the anomaly as a network packet loss fault through multi-dimensional time-series pattern analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we input the model inference results along with system context information into the RCA expert LLM and compare its output with that of an LLM fed directly with raw data, as shown in Fig.<a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-E RQ3: Ablation Study ‣ IV Evaluation ‣ TAMO:Fine-Grained Root Cause Analysis via Tool-Assisted LLM Agent with Multi-Modality Observation Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. This framework leverages tool information and domain expertise to accurately analyze current system faults and provide reasonable remediation suggestions. In contrast, the normal LLM using only raw data struggles to handle the vast amount of observational data due to severe context limitations, resulting in significant omissions and misjudgments in root cause identification and fault classification, as well as corresponding errors in the proposed remediation suggestions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces an innovative Tool-Assisted LLM Agent framework named TAMO to address the complex challenges of root cause analysis in cloud-native microservice environments. By integrating domain-specific tools with large language models, TAMO effectively handles high-dimensional, multi-modal cloud-native observability data and accurately pinpoints fault origins amidst dynamically changing service dependencies. The approach not only overcomes the information loss issues when converting raw observability data into a format suitable for LLM processing but also addresses the challenge that static pre-trained knowledge struggles to adapt to real-time changes in service dependencies. Moreover, by structuring and distilling key results, it guides the LLM to generate contextually appropriate remediation strategies, thereby avoiding limitations imposed by fixed-length context windows.
Our research demonstrates that using a dual-branch collaborative diffusion model enables capturing consistent patterns across different data modalities, providing a unified temporal representation for downstream analysis tasks. Additionally, the root cause localization method based on frequency-domain attention mechanisms enhances the accuracy of identifying causal dependency patterns while effectively filtering out low-frequency noise, achieving precise fault localization.
The experimental results show that, compared to existing advanced methods and benchmarks, TAMO performs notably better on two standard datasets, highlighting its potential to improve fault diagnosis efficiency and accuracy. Further ablation studies and case analyses also validate the effectiveness of each component of the framework and the superior performance of their collaborative work. However, as system scale and complexity continue to grow, how to more efficiently process larger volumes of data streams and further enhance real-time interpretability and cross-modal semantic alignment remain critical areas for future research.</p>
</div>
</section>
